Start the audio and video tracks together on a transcoded stream.

Recording the output of three profiles with curl and reading the Matroska
blocks directly shows the audio track beginning well before the first picture:

    profile      video starts   audio starts   offset
    software        534 ms          0 ms       + 534 ms
    vaapi          1391 ms          0 ms       +1391 ms
    qsv            1368 ms          0 ms       +1368 ms

After that the streams are clean: zero gaps above 50 ms across all three
recordings, frame spacing a steady 33-34 ms. The only anomaly is the start.

The delay is not in the transcoder. Timing the logs from the moment the codec
profile is picked:

    profile      filter graph    first frame   dimension errors
    software        +509 ms        +538 ms           0
    vaapi          +1193 ms       +1216 ms          27
    qsv            +1244 ms       +1331 ms           0

Only 23 to 87 ms separate the filter graph being configured from the first
frame coming out of it. Everything before that is the MPEG-2 decoder waiting
for a sequence header from the source, which is where the width and height come
from; until it arrives the decoder rejects every picture it is handed
("AVCodecContext: Invalid frame dimensions 0x0" -- mpeg12dec.c, emitted on the
VAAPI run only, but the same wait happens silently on the others).

Meanwhile the audio encoder is producing packets from the first moment, so
globalheaders releases its hold queue with several hundred milliseconds of
audio ahead of the first picture. The client plays sound over a blank or frozen
frame until video catches up.

This patch drops the audio that precedes the first picture when the queue is
released. Nothing is delayed, no video is touched, and an audio-only
subscription (no video in the queue at all) is released unchanged.

Measured, four recordings per profile. Offset between the first audio packet
and the first picture:

    profile      before        after (4 runs)             median
    software     534 ms        18, 91, 169, 226 ms        130 ms
    vaapi       1391 ms        269, 283, 430, 1052 ms     356 ms
    qsv         1368 ms        78, 78, 92, 110 ms          85 ms

QSV is consistent, software much improved. One VAAPI run came back at 1052 ms,
essentially the pre-patch figure: this patch filters the hold queue only, and
gh_passthru is armed the moment that queue drains, so audio arriving between
the release and the first picture still slips out ahead of it. Patch 108
extends the same filter past the release and closes that second window; the two
are meant to be measured separately.

Final measurement, five runs per encoder, offset between the first audio packet
and the first picture (negative = picture first, which is what a viewer wants):

    step                  SOFTWARE        VAAPI          QSV
                          med / worst     med / worst    med / worst
    none                   534 / 534      1391 / 1391    1368 / 1368
    107 alone              130 / 226       356 / 1052       85 / 110
    107 + 108 (flag)       280 / 295       304 /  385       53 /  93
    107 + 108 (pts)        -11 /   -5       -9 /    0      -18 /   -2

Fifteen measurements in the final row, every one at or below zero. See
106-108-av-start-HOWTO.txt for how the three patches divide the problem.

The trade-off. Discarding leading audio means
the stream starts at the first picture and loses up to ~1.4 s of sound that the
source did provide. The alternative is to hold the audio instead of dropping
it, so nothing is lost but start-up latency grows by the same amount. Which is
right depends on whether a live stream should favour immediacy or completeness
-- a judgement for the transcoding design rather than for packaging.

A third option addresses the cause rather than the symptom: do not build the
transcode chain until elementary_info_t carries the geometry, or hold packets
back from the decoder until the first sequence header. That would not shorten
the wait, which comes from the source, but it would stop 27 doomed packets
being pushed through the decoder first.

Not introduced by PR #2117: globalheaders.c is untouched by it.

--- src/plumbing/globalheaders.c.orig
+++ src/plumbing/globalheaders.c
@@ -440,13 +440,52 @@ gh_hold(globalheaders_t *gh, streaming_message_t *sm)
 				   streaming_start_copy(gh->gh_ss));
     streaming_target_deliver2(gh->gh_output, sm);
 
-    // Send all pending packets
-    while((pkt = pktref_get_first(&gh->gh_holdq)) != NULL) {
-      if (pkt->pkt_payload) {
-        sm = streaming_msg_create_pkt(pkt);
-        streaming_target_deliver2(gh->gh_output, sm);
+    /* The hold queue usually starts with audio: on a transcoded stream the
+       audio encoder produces its first packet while the video side is still
+       waiting for the source to deliver a sequence header, so video only joins
+       0.5 to 1.5 s later. Releasing the queue as-is hands the client a stream
+       whose audio begins well before its first picture -- measured on one
+       source: audio at 0 ms against video at 534 ms (software), 1391 ms
+       (VAAPI), 1368 ms (QSV).
+
+       Drop the audio that precedes the first picture so both tracks start
+       together. Nothing is delayed and no video is touched; only leading audio
+       with nothing to show alongside it is discarded. If the queue holds no
+       video at all -- an audio-only subscription -- everything is released
+       unchanged. */
+    {
+      th_pktref_t *pr;
+      int64_t first_video_pts = PTS_UNSET;
+
+      TAILQ_FOREACH(pr, &gh->gh_holdq, pr_link) {
+        ssc = streaming_start_component_find_by_index(gh->gh_ss,
+                                        pr->pr_pkt->pkt_componentindex);
+        if (ssc && SCT_ISVIDEO(ssc->es_type)) {
+          first_video_pts = pr->pr_pkt->pkt_pts;
+          break;
+        }
+      }
+
+      // Send all pending packets
+      while((pkt = pktref_get_first(&gh->gh_holdq)) != NULL) {
+        if (pkt->pkt_payload) {
+          int skip = 0;
+          if (first_video_pts != PTS_UNSET) {
+            ssc = streaming_start_component_find_by_index(gh->gh_ss,
+                                            pkt->pkt_componentindex);
+            skip = ssc && !SCT_ISVIDEO(ssc->es_type) &&
+                   pkt->pkt_pts != PTS_UNSET &&
+                   pkt->pkt_pts < first_video_pts;
+          }
+          if (skip) {
+            pkt_trace(LS_GLOBALHEADERS, pkt, "drop, precedes first picture");
+          } else {
+            sm = streaming_msg_create_pkt(pkt);
+            streaming_target_deliver2(gh->gh_output, sm);
+          }
+        }
+        pkt_ref_dec(pkt);
       }
-      pkt_ref_dec(pkt);
     }
     gh->gh_passthru = 1;
     break;
